    // line 28
    vela_builder_add(v_out, VELA_STR("## Syntax and values\012", 21));
    // line 29
    vela_builder_add(v_out, VELA_STR("\012", 1));
    // line 30
    vela_builder_add(v_out, VELA_STR("Files use `.vl`. A program starts at `fn main()` with no parameters or return type. Libraries need no main. Comments begin with `//`. Newlines end statements; a newline after an operator continues the expression. A function's opening `{` belongs with its signature (or its final contract clause). Use parentheses to disambiguate a struct literal in an if/for/match condition. There are no classes or methods: call `module.function(value)`.\012", 440));
    // line 31
    vela_builder_add(v_out, VELA_STR("\012", 1));
    // line 32
    vela_builder_add(v_out, VELA_STR("`Int` is a signed 64-bit integer; arithmetic and indexing are checked and panic on overflow or an invalid index. `Byte` ranges from 0 to 255 and is written `'a'` or `'\\n'`; `Int` and `Byte` require explicit conversion. `Float` uses 64-bit floating point, with literals such as `1.5`; `float(n)` and `int(x)` convert. `Bool` is `true` or `false`. `Str` is UTF-8 text in double quotes with escapes `\\n \\t \\r \\0 \\\\ \\\"`. `Unit` has the value `()`. There are no implicit numeric conversions.\012", 487));
    // line 33
    vela_builder_add(v_out, VELA_STR("\012", 1));
    // line 34
    vela_builder_add(v_out, VELA_STR("`+ - * / %` do arithmetic; `+` also joins two strings. `== != < <= > >=` compare; `&& || !` operate on Bool and short-circuit. Precedence, from tight to loose: calls/fields/index/`\?`, unary `- !`, `* / %`, `+ -`, comparisons, `&&`, `||`. There is no truthiness, ternary operator, increment operator or compound assignment. Write `n = n + 1`.\012", 342));
    // line 35
    vela_builder_add(v_out, VELA_STR("\012", 1));
    // line 36
    vela_builder_add(v_out, VELA_STR("`let x = value` infers a type; `let x: Int = 1` annotates it. `let mut x = value` allows reassignment and changes to its fields/elements. Values have copy semantics: assignment, arguments, returns and closure captures copy logically. Arrays/maps/strings share storage until a change, then copy. Changing a copy never changes the original. String elements cannot be assigned. Struct fields use `x.field`.\012", 404));
    // line 37
    vela_builder_add(v_out, VELA_STR("\012", 1));
    // line 38
    vela_builder_add(v_out, VELA_STR("`[1, 2]` is an array `[Int]`. Annotate an empty array: `let xs: [Int] = []`. Indexes start at 0. `xs[a..b]` copies an array slice; `s[a..b]` shares immutable string bytes. Both exclude b; strings index to Byte and positions count bytes. `len(s)` counts bytes. `for x in xs` visits elements; `for i in 0..n` excludes n; use an explicit bound for an inclusive loop. In match patterns, `a..=b` includes b. `for condition { ... }` is a while loop; `for { ... }` repeats indefinitely. `break`, `continue`, `return value`, and `return` behave as named. `if condition { ... } else if ... else { ... }` controls statements; use a match expression for conditional values.\012", 663));
    // line 39
    vela_builder_add(v_out, VELA_STR("\012", 1));
    // line 40
    vela_builder_add(v_out, VELA_STR("A map is `Map[K, V]`; keys satisfy Key. Write `let mut m: Map[Str, Int] = [:]` or `[\"a\": 1]`. `m[key] = value` inserts/replaces; `m[key]` panics if absent. Use maps.get for optional lookup. `for key in maps.keys(m)` visits keys in insertion order. Maps and arrays are values: pass them with `mut` to change a caller's copy.\012", 324));
    // line 41
